course: dictionary
lesson: 1516
title: Official counts and special words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have a simple meaning in a story and a more exact meaning in a subject like maths, geography or history. Context helps you find the exact meaning. «Mary and Joseph travelled to Bethlehem for a Roman census, a count of all the people» tells you straight away what a census is: a count of everyone.
explain: Look for the phrases around the word. «Take a census» and «census data» are phrases you will meet in maths and geography. Census data is the set of facts and numbers that a census gives, such as how many people live in a place. Those numbers are often shown in graphs and tables.
explain: The word can also be used for other counts. A bird census is an official count of birds. Always check by swapping your meaning into the sentence: "a count of all the people" fits in the first example, so census means exactly that.
fact: Census means an official count, usually of all the people in a country.
fact: A census can also be an official count of animals or birds, such as a bird census.
fact: A census often gives facts about people, such as their ages and jobs.
fact: «Census data» shows how many people live in a place, and it is used in graphs and tables.
fact: «Domesday Book» of 1086 was a count of land and households made for taxes, so it was an unusual record for its time.
exercise: «Mary and Joseph travelled to Bethlehem for a Roman census, a count of all the people.» What is a census? | A room at an inn | A long journey by boat | A kind of Roman food | *A count of all the people
exercise: Which of these is an example of a census of animals? | A recipe for soup | A map of a wood | *A bird census, an official count of birds | A list of your favourite songs
exercise: In maths or geography, what does «census data» show? | The colour of the sky | The name of an old king | *How many people live in a place | How to bake bread
test: In «the baby was laid in a manger, an animal feeding trough», what is a manger? | A shepherd's coat | A type of boat | *An animal feeding trough | A room at an inn
test: Why are the population numbers for EU5 in 1337 only estimates? | The numbers were copied from a boat | The people did not want to be counted | The numbers are written in gold | *There were few real censuses, official counts of the people, in 1337
exam: What does census mean? | A journey to a far country | *An official count of people, with facts such as their ages and jobs | A sort of castle wall | A kind of bread
